Z||000000||fierobob||06-03-2000||08:08 PM||||just had my 88 fiero ac system retrofited to<BR>134a. compressor was locked up and figured<BR>while I was having the compresor replaced it<BR>would be a good time to do it. After driving about 20 miles the new compressor locked up.<BR>work was done at pep boys,according to paper<BR>work the parts they replaced were dryer, ac<BR>compressor, orafice tube, installed retrofit<BR>kit and 8.5oz oil. they said in phone conversation that debrie may have caused it<BR>Does'nt ofafice tube keep debrie<BR> out of compressor?<BR>Also rad. ran runs all the time now.<BR>any ideas on what happened<P>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||86se/lambo||06-04-2000||02:37 AM||||when i did my retrofit i noticed the expansion valve was black and completely clogged.. i figured thats probably why the compressor ended up failing..though i'm not sure where all the debree came from. i borrowed the advice of another member and flushed the lines with denatured alcohol.my compressor already had the r-134 oil, so i only added half of the 8 oz. bottle ( i had an a/c guy do this). when i got it home the fan ran all the time and the copressor cycled on and off rapidly(every 20-30 seconds or so). I finally figured it out, the fan deal was the compressor i put in was the da6 and the one that blew was a v5. the v5 has 2 connections on the back of the compressor(besides the clutch)and the da6 has only one. anyway if this happened to you make sure the black wire is hooked up to the high side-high pressure switch(the only terminal on back of the da6)the green white i left disconnected and strapped it out of the way(it was used as a high side low pressure switch on the v5 compressor). For the quick cycling problem i removed the low pressure wire connector at the accumalator(reciever drier)and turned the adjusting screw about an eighth to a quarter of a turn counterclockwise and that did the trick.hopefully pep boys will replace that compressor for no charge, if not you might want to buy one with a lifetime gurantee (napa). dang..i'm starting to babblehope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||06-04-2000||02:39 AM||||Twenty miles.  Law is on your side unless they can show hammer marks and a hammer with your fingerprints on it.<P>You are correct, debris did not cause this(unless they put it there), somebody or some thing screwed up.  If it's the unit itself Pep Boys can get their money back. If they forgot to suck out the old oil (or somesuch) then they get to eat it, but you paid for a retrofit which includes making sure everything works correctly before releasing it as ready.<P>The fan thinks the AC unit is turned on all the time.<P>I know I just hate it when a rookie gets his on the job training on something of mine.  Reality, he's gotta learn somewhere.  But, you do not have to pay for his learning experiences.  <P>Sugar and vinegar, this is a chain, keep going up the links, firmly and politely until an answer is gotten.  The Better Business Bureau often helps get the correct answer. ||||reg||12
